==Latin==

===Etymology===
From {{proto|Indo-European|deyḱ-e-|to show, point out|lang=la}}. Cognates include Oscan {{term||deíkum|to show, point out}}, Sanskrit {{term|sc=Deva|दिशति|tr=diśáti|lang=sa}}, Ancient Greek {{term|sc=polytonic|δείκνυμι|tr=deiknumi|lang=grc}} and Old English {{term|tæcan|tǣċan|lang=ang}} (English {{term|teach|lang=ang}}).

====Pronunciation====
{{rfc-level|Pronunciation at L4+ not in L3 POS section|lang=la}}
* {{IPA|lang=la|/ˈdiː.koː/|lang=la}}

====Verb====
{{la-verb|dico|dīcō|dicere|dīcere|dixi|dīxī|dictum|dictum}}

# I [[say]], [[speak]], [[talk]].
#: '''Dixit''' duas res ei rubori fuisse.
#:: ''He '''said''' that two things had abashed him.''
# I [[affirm]], [[declare]], [[state]].
# I [[tell]], [[call]].
#* '''c. 254–184 BCE''', [[w:Plautus|Plautus]], [[w:Captivi|Captivi]] ("The Captives"), 623, ([http://www.ihaystack.com/authors/m/plautus_titus_maccius/00016564_amphitryo_asinaria_aulularia_bacchides_captivi_amphi/00016564_englishlatin_utf8_p043.htm English and Latin text])
#*: Eho '''dic''' mihi, quis illic igitur est?
#*:: ''Hey? '''Tell''' me, who is he then?''

